what The rostered fleet_claim_form guard is RED on every ship right now and has been since 20260917.143729: construction/fleet-claims.kyri line 55 carries a phantom record named against, the 952-byte tail of a closed claim's own what sentence, reading bad_names=1 and missing_fields=5 for verdict=malformed. Measured before claiming rather than reasoned: the writer's own --close is NOT the cause. Replaying tools/f/fleet_claim.sh's close awk against the pre-close board on metal produces the landed file byte-for-byte MINUS that line, so the cut was a hand's, and its terminator was the unanchored literal claim followed by a space, which the closed record's own sentence carried in the phrase neither orders the claim against the cold run. The sharper half is that a second reader disagrees with the first about one board and nobody hears it: tools/fixtures/f/fleet_claim_scan.sh, the reader EVERY ship runs before EVERY build, prints form=readable corrupting=0 over this same board and answers verdict=clear. Readable is its own word rather than the form scan's, and on a board whose form verdict is malformed it is false. The split that keeps confined findings from refusing is PROVEN and stays untouched -- a confined finding must not red a peer's board, and that leg is asserted in the form witness. What changes is one summary line: the content reader carries the form scan's own verdict and its confined count forward instead of substituting a word of its own, so a ship reading the board before it builds learns the same thing the rostered guard knows. This lap repairs the phantom line in the same commit as the claim so the fleet-wide red closes an hour sooner, names it by stamp, and books one ledger row. Touches no module source, no ceiling any peer watches, no roster row, and no peer-claimed path: fleet_claim_scan.sh's summary line, the form control's pen, the board's one dead line, and the ledger.
